From 1f6f36f0c85117299dd833cd44aa46bc1cc76745 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michal Frdlik <michaelf.ms@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 22:39:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Thylacine Warp-3: the virgl winsys over the /srv/warp GPU
 seam

The unmodified virgl gallium driver, carried by a new winsys
(src/gallium/winsys/virgl/thylacine/) whose transport is warp_client.c:
blocking file ops on the tapestryd-served 9P tree via raw Thylacine
syscalls (<thyla/syscall.h>, carried by the generated cross file's
c_args). The vtest backend's 18 load-bearing slots, minus every
displaytarget arm -- the OSMesa frontend reads back through pipe
transfers, so flush_frontbuffer stays NULL (guarded upstream).

Fences are counted, not parsed: the client counts fenced ops it ISSUED
(submits + transfers, FIFO per ring) against the seam's monotonic
per-ctx `fence-signaled` counter; the fence FILE is only the blocking
primitive (its coalesced record content is ignored) and is never read
with nothing in flight, so no park is unfillable. transfer_get is
synchronous (queue + fence-wait) because the driver maps and reads on
return -- the same choice vtest's protocol-v2 arm makes. Submits split
at CCMD boundaries into <= 24 KiB writes (one Twrite = one atomic
submission; msize is 32 KiB and past the iounit the kernel client
would fragment mid-command); a single command wider than the bound
fails loudly and latches (GPU-DESIGN 4.1's Loom bulk path is the
successor). Hard failures LATCH the connection: the driver ignores
submit_cmd's return, so a dropped command buffer must not be silent.

Build wiring keys on `with_thylacine = cc.get_define('__thylacine__')`
-- the cross file deliberately claims system='linux', so the compiler
is the only honest meson-level signal (and it is the same one
detect_os.h keys on). On thylacine the virgl driver drops dep_libdrm
(zero xf86drm includes; its drm-uapi headers are the in-tree copies)
and builds this winsys instead of drm+vtest. The osmesa target grows
the virgl closure via link_whole (build.sh links by hand) and selects
the screen at runtime on GALLIUM_DRIVER=virpipe|virgl, falling back
loudly to the software rasteriser.

virgl_prove.c is the Warp-3 gate binary: link proof on the builder,
triangle-on-the-GPU prover in-guest. Discrimination is structural
twice over: it never walks the CAP_JIT clearance (llvmpipe cannot run
without it, so a fallback cannot paint the triangle) and GL_RENDERER
must name virgl and not be bare llvmpipe. Exit 0/2/1 = PASS/SKIP/FAIL.

The virgl disk-cache keying asserted a build-id note into existence; a
static link that never passed --build-id has none, so the assert
aborted screen creation in-guest at first tri-gate contact. The keying
contribution is now conditional: no note -> return with disk_cache
NULL, the exact configuration MESA_SHADER_CACHE_DISABLE ships, which
every consumer already handles (the getter returns NULL and
disk_cache_destroy is NULL-guarded). llvmpipe's util-path identifier
fallback degrades the same way.
